This is the Final Boss Level for Relationships. 🏆
If you can build this, you have officially mastered how to mix Single Objects (@OneToOne) and Lists (@OneToMany) in the same project.
🎓 Project: "University Management System"
The Scenario:
You are building the backend for student admissions.
 * The Rule of 1: Every student gets exactly ONE ID Card (One-to-One).
 * The Rule of Many: Every student can have MANY Certificates (One-to-Many).
1️⃣ The Input (JSON Structure)
Notice the difference?
 * "idCard" is a curly brace { } (Single Object).
 * "certificates" is a square bracket [ ] (List of Objects).
Input (StudentRequest):
{
    "studentName": "Rahul",
    "email": "rahul@uni.com",
    
    "idCard": {
        "cardNumber": "UNI-2024-001",
        "expiryDate": "2028-05-01"
    },

    "certificates": [
        { "courseName": "Java Bootcamp", "grade": "A" },
        { "courseName": "Spring Boot",   "grade": "B+" }
    ]
}

Output (StudentResponse):
{
    "studentId": 1,
    "studentName": "Rahul",
    "idCardNumber": "UNI-2024-001",
    "totalCertificates": 2,
    "status": "ADMITTED"
}

2️⃣ The Entities (Database Design)
Class A: IDCard (Child 1)
 * Fields: id, cardNumber, expiryDate.
Class B: Certificate (Child 2)
 * Fields: id, courseName, grade.
Class C: Student (The Parent)
 * Fields: id, studentName, email.
 * The Challenge: You need TWO relationships here.
   * @OneToOne for IDCard.
   * @OneToMany for List<Certificate>.
3️⃣ The Logic (Service Layer) 🧠
Inside createStudent:
 * Create Student object.
 * Set Name and Email.
 * Handle the Single Object: student.setIdCard(request.getIdCard());
 * Handle the List: student.setCertificates(request.getCertificates());
 * Save (studentRepo.save(student)).
 * Return Response.
4️⃣ Your Task Checklist ✅
Write the code for:
 * 3 Entities: Student, IDCard, Certificate.
 * 2 DTOs: StudentRequest, StudentResponse.
 * 1 Repository: StudentRepo.
 * 1 Service: StudentService.
Show me the full code. Use this to prove you know the difference between a variable and a list! 🚀
